# Project Rules

<version>1.1.0</version>

When applying this rule prefix your response with [Project Manager]

## Project Structure

### Main Project
- **Primary application**: `website/` - Main Next.js platform
- **Entry point**: Main user-facing application and orchestration layer

### Package Architecture  
- **Subprojects**: `packages/` - Individual feature modules
- **Dual Mode Design**:
  - **Standalone**: Each package runs independently with own `src/*` directory
  - **Composition**: Packages built for import/integration into main platform
- **Individual Structure**: Each package has complete `src/app/`, `src/components/`, `src/lib/` structure

## Key Technologies
- **Next.js 15**: App Router, React Server Components
- **TypeScript**: Strict typing across all layers  
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS (default choice)

## Important Guidelines
1. **DO NOT reinvent existing functionality** - Use built-in features, auth, and established patterns
2. **Kill existing dev servers before builds** when needed:
   ```bash
   lsof -ti:3000 | xargs kill -9 && lsof -ti:5555 | xargs kill -9
   ```
3. **Respect project structure**:
   - `website/src/app/` - Main platform pages/components
   - `packages/[name]/src/app/` - Package-specific pages (standalone mode)
   - `packages/[name]/src/components/` - Package components (exportable)
   - `packages/[name]/src/lib/` - Package utilities (exportable)
   - `app/api/` - Server-side API routes
   - `components/` - Reusable UI components
   - `lib/` - Shared utilities and helpers
   - `types/` - TypeScript type definitions

## Development Workflow
- Use established development commands
- Follow existing code formatting and linting rules
- Maintain test coverage for new features
- Use TypeScript strictly - avoid `any` types
- Consider both standalone and composition use cases when developing packages

## Architecture Patterns
- Server Components by default, Client Components when needed
- API routes for server-side logic
- Proper separation of concerns
- Follow established authentication patterns
- Design packages for both independent operation and platform integration

## AI Model Preferences
- OpenAI: Use established model configuration
- Follow API abstraction patterns  
- Implement proper error handling and rate limiting
